n  terms of personal protective equipment  things like surgical masks gloves the  gowns that these frontline First  Responder health care workers need to  deal with coronavirus and so the new  concern is this national stockpile the  strategic national stockpile is as it's  known essentially what we're being told  is that 90 percent of the stockpile has  been dispersed and they're not going to  give any more because they're keeping  ten percent for the federal government  in its response so where is it going  it's going to state and local  governments now this is a criticism that  has Democrats on Capitol Hill  particularly unhappy they're pointing  the Trump administration saying you're  in charge of the federal response and  what you've left these states and local  governments to do now is to essentially  get into bidding war scouring the open  market and in fact we've seen even on  social media frontline workers taking  pictures of themselves wearing garbage  bags in place out these protective gowns  that normally they would use now the  Trump administration is defending its  response saying it's the fault of the  states they should have been better  prepared but I can tell you it's not  just the in terms of shortages in the  United States it's not exclusive to just  personal protective equipment in fact  ordinary Americans are finding when they  go to the grocery stores things they  count on like toilet paper even canned  goods are also scarce are hard to find  even not at all so this is an issue that  is ongoing and as I pointed out again  already there is this finger pointing  towards the Trump administration so far  it is deflecting blame all right it's a  clearly difficult situation than the the  latest unemployment numbers only add to  that what is the Federal Reserve  planning to do about it  well the Federal Reserve is a body that  is a monetary body in terms of interest  rates and the rest but in terms of the  response with regard to personal  protective equipment this is something  that is an ongoing crisis now if you  watch the briefings in the White House  briefing room they will tell you that  everything's great it's under control  we've never seen something like this  before and we're doing the best we can  but I can tell you we've seen some  unusual characters popping in to take  control of this situation for example  the president's son-in-law Jared Kushner  who obviously has no expertise when it  comes to dispatching equipment to in  emergency situations his real estate  background certainly does not make him  especially equip for handling this  crisis and yet that's one of the the  people that the president now has  overseeing this and in fact he did have  a bit of a slip up this week in terms of  even understanding the basics of how the  the strategic reserve worked and as a  result there's been a lot of criticism  that this is an administration that  simply doesn't know what it's doing and  it is evident in terms of the lack of  materials that are out in the states and  local governments now all right Kimberly  haki there thanks for that
 

